 at most one point per column.\n\nWe can think of each line as a function mapping column index to a value. For each line i, we can define its values at columns 2,3,4,5 as v_i2, v_i3, v_i4, v_i5 (some may be outside allowed range). The condition that the line is linear means that for any two columns c1,c2 where the line has assigned points, the slope m_i = (v_i(c2) - v_i(c1)) / (c2 - c1) must be consistent across all assigned columns. So each line's assigned points must lie on a line.\n\nThus we need to partition the set of points into at most 4 subsets, each subset being collinear (non-vertical, non-horizontal, non-slope -1). Additionally, each subset cannot contain two points from same column (since that would be vertical). So each subset is a set of points with distinct x-coordinates.\n\nThus we need to partition the points into at most 4 subsets, each subset being a set of points with distinct x-coordinates that lie on a line with slope not 0, ∞, -1.\n\nWe can try to find such a partition manually.\n\nPoints:\n\n(2,1)\n(2,2)\n(2,3)\n(2,4)\n\n(3,1)\n(3,2)\n(3,3)\n\n(4,1)\n(4,2)\n\n(5,1)\n\nGoal: partition into 4 subsets.\n\nOne approach: Use lines that go through (2,1)-(3,2)-(4,3)-(5,4) but (4,3) not allowed. So we can have line L1: (2,1)-(3,2) (slope 1). That's line y = x - 1. It also passes through (4,3) (outside) and (5,4) (outside). So L1 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nLine L2: (2,2)-(3,3) (slope 1). That's line y = x. It passes through (4,4) (outside) and (5,5) (outside). So L2 covers (2,2) and (3,3). Good.\n\nLine L3: (2,3)-(4,1) (slope (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So can't use that.\n\nLine L3: (2,3)-(5,1) (slope (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3). That's sunny. It also passes through (3,5/3) not integer, (4,? ) = (1-? )? Let's compute: y = -2/3 x + b. Using (2,3): 3 = -4/3 + b => b = 3 + 4/3 = 13/3. So line: y = -2/3 x + 13/3. At x=4, y = -8/3 + 13/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666, not integer. So L3 covers (2,3) and (5,1). Good.\n\nLine L4: (2,4)-(4,2) (slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So can't use that.\n\nAlternative: (2,4)-(3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, sunny. That's line y = -3x + b. Using (2,4): 4 = -6 + b => b = 10. So line: y = -3x + 10. At x=3, y = -9 + 10 = 1, good. At x=4, y = -12 + 10 = -2 (outside). At x=5, y = -15 + 10 = -5 (outside). So L4 covers (2,4) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered points:\n\nL1: (2,1),(3,2)\nL2: (2,2),(3,3)\nL3: (2,3),(5,1)\nL4: (2,4),(3,1)\n\nRemaining points: (4,1),(4,2). Also (3,? ) we have covered (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). So only column 4 points left.\n\nWe have used all 4 lines. We cannot add more lines. So we need to cover (4,1) and (4,2) using existing lines. Could any of the existing lines also pass through (4,1) or (4,2)? Let's check:\n\nL1: y = x - 1 => at x=4, y=3, not needed.\nL2: y = x => at x=4, y=4, not needed.\nL3: y = -2/3 x + 13/3 => at x=4, y = -8/3 + 13/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666, not integer.\nL4: y = -3x + 10 => at x=4, y = -12 + 10 = -2, not needed.\n\nThus (4,1) and (4,2) are uncovered. So this partition fails.\n\nWe need to adjust to cover column 4 points. Perhaps we can assign some column 4 points to lines that also cover column 2 points, but we need to keep each line sunny.\n\nLet's try to incorporate column 4 points into lines that already have points in columns 2 and 3.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each can have up to 4 points (one per column). So we need to assign each column's points across lines. Let's try to assign column 4 points to lines L1 and L2 perhaps.\n\nWe need to find lines that pass through (4,1) and some point in column 2 or 3, and also not be horizontal, vertical, slope -1.\n\nOption: line through (4,1) and (2,2): slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, sunny. Equation: y - 1 = -1/2 (x - 4) => y = -1/2 x + 3. At x=2, y = -1 + 3 = 2, good. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 3 = 1.5, not integer. So line L5: (2,2)-(4,1). Could also pass through (5,0.5) not needed. So we could assign (2,2) to line L5 instead of L2. But L2 currently covers (2,2) and (3,3). We could reassign.\n\nLet's try to design a set of 4 lines that cover all points.\n\nWe need to cover 10 points with 4 lines, each line can cover at most 4 points (since 4 columns). So total capacity is 16, enough. But we need to find a partition.\n\nLet's try to use a systematic approach: assign each line to a pair of points in columns 2 and 5 maybe, and also include column 4 points.\n\nWe have columns 2,3,4,5. Let's denote lines L1-L4.\n\nGoal: each line picks at most one point per column.\n\nWe need to cover column 5 point (5,1). So at least one line must pass through (5,1). Let's denote that line as L1.\n\nL1 passes through (5,1) and some point in column 2,3,4. Let's try to pick (2,4) as we did earlier: slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So can't use (2,4). Try (2,3): slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, sunny. So L1: (2,3)-(5,1). Good.\n\nNow L1 also could pass through a point in column 4 maybe? Let's compute L1 equation: y = -2/3 x + b. Using (2,3): 3 = -4/3 + b => b = 13/3. So L1: y = -2/3 x + 13/3. At x=4, y = -8/3 + 13/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666, not integer. So L1 does not cover column 4 integer point.\n\nThus L1 covers (2,3) and (5,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover column 2 points (2,1),(2,2),(2,4) and column 3 points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) and column 4 points (4,1),(4,2). We have 3 lines left: L2, L3, L4.\n\nWe need to assign each remaining point to one of these lines, ensuring each line is linear and not horizontal, vertical, slope -1.\n\nLet's try to pair points across columns.\n\nOne approach: Use line L2: (2,1)-(3,2)-(4,3?) but (4,3) not needed. So L2: (2,1)-(3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, sunny. So L2: y = x - 1. It covers (2,1),(3,2). Good.\n\nNow L3: maybe (2,2)-(3,3) slope = (3-2)/(3-2) = 1, sunny. That's line y = x. It covers (2,2),(3,3). Good.\n\nNow L4: we need to cover (2,4),(3,1),(4,1),(4,2). That's 4 points. Can we find a line that passes through (2,4) and (3,1)? Slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, sunny. Equation: y = -3x + b. Using (2,4): 4 = -6 + b => b = 10. So line: y = -3x + 10. At x=3, y = -9 + 10 = 1, good (covers (3,1)). At x=4, y = -12 + 10 = -2, not (4,1) or (4,2). So L4 does not cover column 4 points.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to cover (4,1) and (4,2) with separate lines, but we only have L4 left. So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nMaybe we can assign (4,1) to L2 or L3? Let's check L2: y = x - 1 => at x=4, y=3, not 1. L3: y = x => at x=4, y=4, not 1 or 2. So not.\n\nThus we need to incorporate column 4 points into some line that also covers a column 2 or 3 point.\n\nLet's try a different assignment.\n\nWe have L1 covering (2,3) and (5,1). L2 could be (2,1)-(4,1) maybe? That's slope = (1-1)/(4-2) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So can't.\n\nL2 could be (2,1)-(4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2, sunny. Equation: y = 0.5 x + b. Using (2,1): 1 = 1 + b => b = 0. So line: y = 0.5 x. At x=4, y=2, good (covers (4,2)). At x=3, y=1.5, not integer. So L2 covers (2,1) and (4,2). Good.\n\nNow L3 could be (2,2)-(3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So can't.\n\nL3 could be (2,2)-(4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, sunny. Equation: y = -0.5 x + b. Using (2,2): 2 = -1 + b => b = 3. So line: y = -0.5 x + 3. At x=4, y = -2 + 3 = 1, good (covers (4,1)). At x=3, y = -1.5 + 3 = 1.5, not integer. So L3 covers (2,2) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow L4 must cover remaining points: (2,4),(3,1),(3,2),(3,3). Actually (3,1) is not covered yet; (3,2) not covered; (3,3) not covered; (2,4) not covered. That's 4 points. Can we find a line that passes through (2,4) and (3,2) maybe? Slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. Equation: y = -2x + b. Using (2,4): 4 = -4 + b => b = 8. So line: y = -2x + 8. At x=3, y = -6 + 8 = 2, good (covers (3,2)). At x=4, y = -8 + 8 = 0 (outside). At x=5, y = -10 + 8 = -2 (outside). So L4 covers (2,4) and (3,2). But we still need (3,1) and (3,3). Could we adjust L4 to also cover (3,1) or (3,3)? No, line can have at most one point per column, so cannot cover both (3,1) and (3,3) if it already covers (3,2). So we need to assign (3,1) and (3,3) to other lines, but those lines already have points assigned: L1 has (2,3) and (5,1); L2 has (2,1) and (4,2); L3 has (2,2) and (4,1). So none of them have a point in column 3 yet. So we could assign (3,1) to L1 maybe? Let's check if L1 passes through (3,1). L1 equation: y = -2/3 x + 13/3. At x=3, y = -2 + 13/3 = ( -6 + 13 )/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not 1. So not.\n\nCould assign (3,1) to L2? L2: y = 0.5 x. At x=3, y = 1.5, not integer.\n\nAssign (3,1) to L3? L3: y = -0.5 x + 3. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 3 = 1.5, not integer.\n\nAssign (3,1) to L4? L4 currently passes through (3,2). Could we change L4 to pass through (3,1) instead? Let's try line through (2,4) and (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, sunny. Equation: y = -3x + b. Using (2,4): 4 = -6 + b => b = 10. So line: y = -3x + 10. At x=3, y = -9 + 10 = 1, good (covers (3,1)). At x=4, y = -12 + 10 = -2 (outside). So L4 covers (2,4) and (3,1). Then (3,2) and (3,3) remain uncovered.\n\nWe could try to assign (3,2) to L2 maybe? L2: y = 0.5 x => at x=3, y=1.5, not integer. L3: y = -0.5 x + 3 => at x=3, y=1.5, not integer. L1: y = -2/3 x + 13/3 => at x=3, y=7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. So none cover (3,2). Could we adjust L2 to cover (3,2) instead of (4,2)? Let's try line through (2,1) and (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, sunny. That's line y = x - 1 (same as earlier L2). At x=4, y=3 (not needed). So L2 could be (2,1)-(3,2). Then we need to cover (4,2) elsewhere. Could assign (4,2) to L3 maybe? L3 currently is (2,2)-(4,1). Could we adjust L3 to cover (4,2) instead? Let's try line through (2,2) and (4,2): slope = (2-2)/(4-2) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So can't.\n\nCould assign (4,2) to L4? L4 currently is (2,4)-(3,1). Could we adjust L4 to also pass through (4,2)? Let's see line through (2,4) and (4,2): slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So can't.\n\nCould assign (4,2) to L1? L1: y = -2/3 x + 13/3 => at x=4, y=5/3 ≈ 1.666, not integer.\n\nThus (4,2) remains uncovered.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe need a different approach.\n\nMaybe we can use a vertical line for column 2 or 3? But we only have one non-sunny line, which we used as vertical x=1 covering column 1. Could we use a non-sunny line that is not vertical, maybe horizontal or slope -1, to cover some points? That might help.\n\nBut the solution's argument for k≥4 uses the fact that each sunny line can meet each column at most one point, and we have at most n-4 vertical lines (since we have k sunny lines, we have n-k non-sunny lines, which can be vertical lines). Actually we have n total lines, k sunny, n-k non-sunny. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. But horizontal lines can cover many points across columns, but they are limited to one row each. Similarly slope -1 lines can cover points across columns but with sum constant.\n\nThus we have n-k non-sunny lines to cover the rest. The solution's argument for k≥4 says that we would run out of vertical lines before covering all columns. But we could use horizontal or slope -1 lines to cover multiple columns. However, horizontal lines can only cover points with same y coordinate. In T_n, each row y = b has points (a,b) for a from 1 to n+1-b. So horizontal lines can cover many points in a row. Similarly slope -1 lines can cover points with a+b = constant.\n\nThus perhaps we can use horizontal lines to cover many points, reducing need for vertical lines. The solution's argument didn't consider using horizontal or slope -1 lines beyond the rightmost columns. So the argument may be incomplete.\n\nThus we need to examine if k≥4 is indeed impossible. Let's try to construct a configuration for n=5, k=4 using a mix of non-sunny lines that are not all vertical. For instance, we could use a horizontal line y=1 covering points (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1). That's a non-sunny line (horizontal). Then we have 3 remaining non-sunny lines (since n=5, k=4, n-k=1 non-sunny line? Wait n=5, k=4 => n-k = 1 non-sunny line. So we have only one non-sunny line. So we cannot have both a vertical and a horizontal line; we have only one non-sunny line. So we must choose either vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. So we cannot use both vertical and horizontal. So we need to decide which non-sunny line is best.\n\nIf we choose a horizontal line y=1, it covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1). Then we need to cover the remaining points: all points with y≥2. That's columns 1-4, rows 2-? Let's list remaining points after covering y=1:\n\nPoints left: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) (since column 1 has points up to y=5? Actually n=5, column 1 has points (1,b) for b ≤ n+1-1 = 5, so (1,2)-(1,5). Column 2: (2,2),(2,3),(2,4). Column 3: (3,2),(3,3). Column 4: (4,2). That's total: column 1: 4 points, column 2: 3 points, column 3: 2 points, column 4: 1 point = 10 points left. We have 4 sunny lines to cover these 10 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so across columns 1-4, each line can cover at most 4 points. So total capacity is 4*4 = 16, enough. But we need to ensure collinearity.\n\nNow we have columns 1-4, each with points as above. Let's try to assign points to 4 lines.\n\nWe need to cover column 1 points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). Since lines are not vertical, they can have at most one point per column, so each of these points must be on a different line. So we need at least 4 lines to cover column 1 points. We have exactly 4 sunny lines, so each line must cover exactly one point in column 1. So each line will have a point in column 1, and possibly points in other columns.\n\nThus we can assign:\n\nLine L1: (1,2) plus maybe other points.\nLine L2: (1,3) plus maybe other points.\nLine L3: (1,4) plus maybe other points.\nLine L4: (1,5) plus maybe other points.\n\nNow we need to cover column 2 points: (2,2),(2,3),(2,4). Each line can have at most one point in column 2. So we need to assign these three points to three of the four lines, leaving one line without a column 2 point.\n\nSimilarly column 3 points: (3,2),(3,3) assign to two lines.\n\nColumn 4 point: (4,2) assign to one line.\n\nThus we need to find lines that pass through assigned points.\n\nLet's try to assign systematically.\n\nWe have 4 lines L1-L4. Let's assign:\n\n- L1: (1,2) and (2,2) and (3,2) and (4,2). That's points (1,2),(2,2),(3,2),(4,2). Are they collinear? Let's check slopes: between (1,2) and (2,2): slope = (2-2)/(2-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So can't have both (1,2) and (2,2) on a sunny line.\n\nThus we need to avoid horizontal lines. So we cannot assign points with same y across columns.\n\nThus we need to assign points such that each line has distinct y values across columns.\n\nLet's try to assign each line to have points with increasing y maybe.\n\nWe have column 1 points with y=2,3,4,5. Column 2 points have y=2,3,4. Column 3 points have y=2,3. Column 4 point has y=2.\n\nWe can try to assign lines as follows:\n\n- L1: (1,2) (col1 y=2), (2,3) (col2 y=3), (3,4?) but column 3 has max y=3, so can't. So maybe L1: (1,2),(2,3),(3,? ) maybe (3,2) but that repeats y=2? Actually L1 already has y=2 at column 1, but can have y=2 at column 3? That would be two points with same y but different x, which would be horizontal line if they share same y across columns? Actually if a line passes through (1,2) and (3,2), slope = (2-2)/(3-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So cannot have same y across any two points on a sunny line. So each line must have distinct y values across all points it contains.\n\nThus each line must have a strictly monotonic y (or at least not constant). So we need to assign points such that each line's y values are all distinct.\n\nGiven column 1 y values are 2,3,4,5, we can assign each line a distinct y from column 1. Then for each other column, we need to assign a point with a y different from the line's column 1 y.\n\nThus for L1 with (1,2), we cannot assign any other point with y=2. So we cannot assign (2,2),(3,2),(4,2) to L1. So L1 can only take points with y ≠ 2. So possible points: (2,3),(2,4); (3,3); (4,?) only (4,2) which is y=2,