 L2, and avoid L2 having y=1 or s=7. L2 currently has y=1 from (4,1). So we need to avoid giving L2 y=1. So we could assign (4,1) to a different line, maybe L3 or L4, to free L2 from y=1. Let's try a different assignment for column 4.\n\nWe need to reassign column 4 points to L2-L4 such that at least one of them does not have y=1, and also not have s=7 (so that it can take (6,1) later). Actually (6,1) has y=1,s=7. So the line that will take (6,1) must not have y=1 or s=7 already. So we need a line among L2-L4 that currently has neither y=1 nor s=7. Let's try to set up such a line.\n\nWe have three lines L2-L4. Let's aim for L2 to be the line that will take (6,1). So L2 must not have y=1 or s=7 before column 6. So we must assign column 4 point with y=1 to a different line (L3 or L4). Also we must avoid assigning any point with s=7 to L2 before column 6. s=7 points are (4,3) and (5,2) and (6,1). So we must avoid assigning (4,3) to L2. So L2 can take (4,2) (y=2,s=6) or (4,4) (y=4,s=8). But (4,2) is already assigned to L1. So L2 can take (4,4) (y=4,s=8). That's okay (s=8 is okay for L2). So assign L2: (4,4) (y=4,s=8).\n\nNow we need to assign (4,1) and (4,3) to L3 and L4. Let's assign (4,1) to L3 (y=1,s=5) and (4,3) to L4 (y=3,s=7). Then L3 has y=1 (so cannot take (6,1) later), L4 has s=7 (so cannot take (6,1) later). So L2 remains free of y=1 and s=7, good.\n\nNow column 5 points: (5,1)s6, (5,2)s7, (5,3)s8. We need to assign each to a distinct line among L2-L4 (L1 is already assigned (4,2) and will later take (7,1)). L2 currently has (4,4) (y=4,s=8). L3 has (4,1) (y=1,s=5). L4 has (4,3) (y=3,s=7).\n\nNow assign column 5 points:\n\n- (5,1) y=1,s=6. L2 can take y=1? L2 currently has y=4, so y=1 distinct, s=6 distinct from s=8, okay. L3 cannot take y=1 (already has y=1). L4 can take y=1 (distinct from y=3), s=6 distinct from s=7, okay. So (5,1) can go to L2 or L4.\n\n- (5,2) y=2,s=7. L2 can take y=2 (distinct), s=7 distinct from s=8, okay. L3 can take y=2 (distinct), s=7 distinct from s=5, okay. L4 cannot take y=2 (distinct), s=7 repeats (L4 has s=7), cannot. So (5,2) can go to L2 or L3.\n\n- (5,3) y=3,s=8. L2 cannot take y=3 (distinct), s=8 repeats (L2 has s=8), cannot. L3 can take y=3 (distinct), s=8 distinct from s=5, okay. L4 can take y=3 (repeats y=3), cannot. So (5,3) must go to L3.\n\nThus assign (5,3) to L3.\n\nNow L3 has (4,1) (y=1,s=5) and (5,3) (y=3,s=8). y's {1,3} distinct, s's {5,8} distinct.\n\nNow remaining column 5 points: (5,1) and (5,2) to assign to L2 and L4.\n\n- L2 currently has (4,4) (y=4,s=8). L2 can take (5,1) (y=1,s=6) distinct y, s distinct from 8, okay. L2 can also take (5,2) (y=2,s=7) distinct y, s distinct from 8, okay.\n\n- L4 currently has (4,3) (y=3,s=7). L4 can take (5,1) (y=1,s=6) distinct y, s distinct from 7, okay. L4 can take (5,2) (y=2,s=7) s repeats, cannot. So L4 can only take (5,1). So assign (5,1) to L4.\n\nNow L4 has (4,3) (y=3,s=7) and (5,1) (y=1,s=6). y's {3,1} distinct, s's {7,6} distinct.\n\nNow (5,2) must go to L2. Assign (5,2) to L2.\n\nNow L2 has (4,4) (y=4,s=8) and (5,2) (y=2,s=7). y's {4,2} distinct, s's {8,7} distinct.\n\nNow after column 5:\n\n- L1: (4,2) (y=2,s=6)\n- L2: (4,4) (y=4,s=8), (5,2) (y=2,s=7)\n- L3: (4,1) (y=1,s=5), (5,3) (y=3,s=8)\n- L4: (4,3) (y=3,s=7), (5,1) (y=1,s=6)\n\nNow column 6 points: (6,1)s7, (6,2)s8. We need to assign each to a distinct line among L2-L4 (L1 cannot take because it would cause y=1 duplicate later). Actually L1 currently has y=2, s=6. L1 could take (6,1) y=1,s=7 (distinct y, s distinct from 6). But then L1 would have y=1, and later (7,1) would cause duplicate y=1. So L1 cannot take (6,1). L1 cannot take (6,2) because y=2 repeats. So L1 must be empty for column 6.\n\nNow assign (6,1) and (6,2) to L2, L3, L4.\n\nCheck each line:\n\n- L2: y {4,2}, s {8,7}. (6,1) y=1 distinct, s=7 repeats (L2 has s=7 from (5,2)), cannot. (6,2) y=2 repeats (L2 has y=2), cannot.\n\n- L3: y {1,3}, s {5,8}. (6,1) y=1 repeats, cannot. (6,2) y=2 distinct, s=8 repeats (L3 has s=8 from (5,3)), cannot.\n\n- L4: y {3,1}, s {7,6}. (6,1) y=1 repeats, cannot. (6,2) y=2 distinct, s=8 distinct (L4 has s=7,6). So L4 can take (6,2).\n\nThus assign (6,2) to L4.\n\nNow (6,1) remains unassigned, but no line can take it. So this assignment fails again.\n\nThus we need to adjust earlier assignments to allow a line that can take (6,1). The line that can take (6,1) must have no y=1 and no s=7 before column 6. Let's see which lines currently have those properties.\n\nAfter column 5, we have:\n\n- L2: y {4,2}, s {8,7} (has s=7)\n- L3: y {1,3}, s {5,8} (has y=1)\n- L4: y {3,1}, s {7,6} (has y=1 and s=7)\n\nThus none of L2-L4 are free of both y=1 and s=7. So we need to adjust assignments to create a line without y=1 and s=7.\n\nWe can try to change column 5 assignments to avoid giving s=7 to L2 and y=1 to L3 or L4. Let's see if we can assign (5,2) (y=2,s=7) to L3 instead of L2, and assign (5,1) (y=1,s=6) to L2, and assign (5,3) (y=3,s=8) to L4 maybe. Let's explore.\n\nWe need to keep L1 as (4,2). L2 currently (4,4) (y=4,s=8). L3 (4,1) (y=1,s=5). L4 (4,3) (y=3,s=7). We can reassign column 5 points differently.\n\nGoal: create a line (say L2) that has no y=1 and no s=7 after column 5. L2 currently has y=4,s=8. If we assign (5,2) (y=2,s=7) to L2, then L2 would have s=7, not good. So we should avoid giving s=7 to L2. So L2 should take (5,1) (y=1,s=6) or (5,3) (y=3,s=8). But (5,3) s=8 repeats s=8 in L2, not allowed. So L2 can take (5,1) (y=1,s=6). That would give L2 y=1, which we want to avoid for the line that will take (6,1). So maybe L2 is not the line we want to take (6,1). Perhaps L3 could be the line that takes (6,1). L3 currently has y=1, so cannot. L4 currently has s=7, cannot. So we need to restructure column 4 assignments as well.\n\nMaybe we should assign column 4 points differently: we need a line that ends up with no y=1 and no s=7 after column 5. Let's try to assign column 4 points as:\n\n- L2: (4,2) is already taken by L1, so not.\n- L2: (4,1) (y=1,s=5) gives y=1, not good.\n- L2: (4,3) (y=3,s=7) gives s=7, not good.\n- L2: (4,4) (y=4,s=8) is okay (no y=1, no s=7). So L2 should get (4,4) as before.\n\n- L3: we could give (4,1) (y=1,s=5) or (4,2) (already L1) or (4,3) (y=3,s=7). If we give L3 (4,3) (y=3,s=7), then L3 gets s=7, not good. If we give L3 (4,1) (y=1,s=5), then L3 gets y=1, not good. So maybe we should give L3 (4,2) but that's L1. So maybe we need to assign L3 (4,1) and L4 (4,3) as before, but then both L3 and L4 have either y=1 or s=7. So we need to consider that maybe L2 will be the line that takes (6,1). L2 currently has no y=1 and no s=7, so it's a candidate. So we need to ensure L2 does not acquire y=1 or s=7 later. So we must avoid assigning any point with y=1 or s=7 to L2 in columns 5 and 6. Let's see if that's possible.\n\nL2 currently has (4,4) (y=4,s=8). In column 5, points are (5,1) y=1,s=6; (5,2) y=2,s=7; (5,3) y=3,s=8. L2 cannot take (5,1) because y=1 (bad). Cannot take (5,2) because s=7 (bad). Can take (5,3) because s=8 repeats (L2 already has s=8), not allowed. So L2 cannot take any point in column 5! That's a problem because each column must have all points assigned. So L2 must take a point in column 5, but any assignment gives either y=1 or s=7 or s=8 duplicate. So maybe we need to change column 4 assignment to give L2 a different point that allows more flexibility.\n\nLet's try a different column 4 assignment: assign (4,1) to L2, (4,3) to L3, (4,4) to L4, and keep L1 as (4,2). Then L2 has y=1,s=5 (bad for L2 as candidate for (6,1)). L3 has y=3,s=7 (bad). L4 has y=4,s=8 (good). So L4 could be the line that takes (6,1). Let's see if we can make L4 free of y=1 and s=7 after column 5.\n\nL4 currently has (4,4) (y=4,s=8). Good. In column 5, we need to assign points to L2, L3, L4. L4 can take (5,1) (y=1,s=6) (y=1 not allowed if we want L4 to avoid y=1). L4 can take (5,2) (y=2,s=7) (s=7 not allowed). L4 can take (5,3) (y=3,s=8) (s=8 repeats). So L4 cannot take any point in column 5 without violating either y=1 or s=7 or duplicate s. So this assignment fails.\n\nThus we need to consider other possibilities.\n\nMaybe we need to use a different set of non-sunny lines: instead of vertical lines for columns 1-3, we could use a horizontal line y=1 as one of the non-sunny lines, covering many points across columns, reducing the load on sunny lines. But we have only n-k non-sunny lines = 3 for n=7,k=4. We could use vertical lines for columns 1-2 (x=1,x=2) and a horizontal line y=1. That would cover many points, leaving fewer points for sunny lines. Let's explore that.\n\nWe have n=7, k=4, n-k=3 non-sunny lines. Use vertical lines x=1 and x=2, and horizontal line y=1. Then we cover:\n\n- x=1 covers (1,1)-(1,7)\n- x=2 covers (2,1)-(2,6)\n- y=1 covers (1,1)-(7,1) (some overlap with vertical lines)\n\nNow remaining points are those not in columns 1 or 2 and not in row 1. Let's list points in columns 3-7, rows >=2.\n\nColumns:\n\nc=3: points (3,1)-(3,5) originally, but (3,1) covered by y=1, so remaining (3,2),(3,3),(3,4),(3,5). Actually column 3 has points (3,b) for b ≤ n+1-3 = 5, so b=1..5. So after covering (3,1) by y=1, we have (3,2)-(3,5) (4 points).\n\nc=4: points (4,1)-(4,4). (4,1) covered by y=1, remaining (4,2),(4,3),(4,4) (3 points).\n\nc=5: points (5,1)-(5,3). (5,1) covered by y=1, remaining (5,2),(5,3) (2 points).\n\nc=6: points (6,1)-(6,2). (6,1) covered by y=1, remaining (6,2) (1 point).\n\nc=7: point (7,1) covered by y=1, no remaining points.\n\nThus remaining points after non-sunny lines: total = 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. We have k=4 sunny lines to cover these 10 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so across columns 3-6 (4 columns), each line can cover at most 4 points, total capacity 4*4=16, enough. So capacity is fine.\n\nNow we need to assign these points to 4 sunny lines. The points are:\n\nc=3: (3,2)s=5, (3,3)s=6, (3,4)s=7, (3,5)s=8\nc=4: (4,2)s=6, (4,3)s=7, (4,4)s=8\nc=5: (5,2)s=7, (5,3)s=8\nc=6: (6,2)s=8\n\nWe 